# Board versions of the atlas entries. Line: word | group | short logic (<=52) | why this, why not the others (<=62) | tip (<=80)
SHORT = """
and|add|adds an idea that goes the same way|not BUT: nothing goes against the first idea|Do not chain many ANDs; use commas in a list.
but|contrast|second idea goes against the first|not AND: the ideas pull apart; not SO: no result|Never use BUT with ALTHOUGH.
or|choice|a choice, or a warning (or else)|not AND: you cannot have both; not NOR: no negative|OR also means otherwise: a warning.
nor|choice|a second negative, helper verb first|not OR: a negative needs NOR; not AND: it adds a negative|After NOR: nor did he, nor can she.
for|reason|a formal reason, after the main idea|not BECAUSE: for follows a comma and cannot start a sentence|Formal and literary: use because in most writing.
yet|contrast|a surprising contrast, like a strong but|not SO: yet shows surprise, not a result|Avoid "but yet": one is enough.
so|result|the result of what came before|not BECAUSE: so gives the result, because the reason|Never BECAUSE and SO in one sentence.
when|time|at the moment something happens|not WHILE: when is one moment, while is a stretch|Present tense after when: when I arrive.
whenever|time|every time that|not WHEN: whenever means each and every time|Call me whenever you need help.
while|time|during the time that: two things at once|not WHEN: while needs a stretch of time|While can also mean whereas: check the meaning.
as|time|at the same time as a change goes on|not WHILE: as fits a change: as the sun rose|AS has four jobs: time, reason, manner, comparison.
before|time|earlier than another event|not AFTER: before puts the main action first|Present tense after before.
after|time|later than another event|not BEFORE: after puts the main action second|Past perfect helps: after he had eaten.
until|time|up to the moment when|not BEFORE: until means the action goes on up to then|Until and till are the same; until is more formal.
since|time|from a past time up to now|not FOR: since needs a starting point|Use the present perfect: has lived since.
once|time|as soon as one event is done|not WHEN: once stresses that it is finished|Once he understood, he never forgot.
as soon as|time|at the very moment, with no delay|not WHEN: as soon as stresses no delay|Present tense for the future.
by the time|time|not later than that moment|not UNTIL: by the time is a deadline|Use the past perfect: had left.
the moment|time|the exact instant that|not WHEN: the moment is sharper|The moment she saw him, she smiled.
because|reason|the clear, main reason|not SO: because gives the reason, so the result|Because + clause. Because of + noun.
since|reason|a reason the listener already knows|not BECAUSE: since is light, because is the main reason|Since is also a time word: avoid confusion.
as|reason|a light reason, at the start|not BECAUSE: as is weaker and can look like time|Use because if the sentence could be misread.
seeing that|reason|considering the fact that|not BECAUSE: more formal and a little old|Formal.
now that|reason|a new situation is the reason|not BECAUSE: now that needs a recent change|Now that exams are over, we can rest.
inasmuch as|reason|to the extent that (very formal)|not BECAUSE: inasmuch as limits how far it is true|Rare. Use because.
although|concession|main idea still true, in spite of the first|not BUT: although opens the weaker idea; not BECAUSE|One contrast word only.
though|concession|the same as although, may end a sentence|not ALTHOUGH at the end: only though can end one|It is hard. I like it, though.
even though|concession|a stronger although for a real surprise|not EVEN IF: even though is a fact|Stronger than although.
whereas|contrast|sets two plain facts side by side|not ALTHOUGH: there is no surprise, only a difference|Compares two things.
while|contrast|shows that two facts differ|not WHEREAS at the start: while may look like time|Use whereas if you must be clear.
whilst|contrast|the same as while (British, formal)|not WHILE in plain writing|British and formal.
even if|concession|whether or not it happens|not EVEN THOUGH: even if is only a maybe|Even if is about a possibility.
much as|concession|although I like it very much|not ALTHOUGH: much as adds feeling|Formal and elegant.
if|condition|true only when the condition is true|not WHEN: if may never happen|Present tense after if for the future.
unless|condition|if not|not IF: unless means the opposite|Never add not after unless.
provided that|condition|only if: a strict condition|not IF: provided that makes it strict|The same as providing that.
as long as|condition|only if, and while it stays true|not UNTIL: as long as is a condition|Also a time use.
so long as|condition|the same as as long as|not UNLESS: it is a positive condition|So long as you work, you will succeed.
on condition that|condition|formal: only if this is agreed|not IF: it is a legal, formal term|A formal group of words.
in case|condition|be ready, because it may happen|not IF: in case is done before, if is done after|Take an umbrella in case it rains.
supposing|condition|imagine that|not IF: supposing invites imagination|Supposing it rains, what shall we do?
only if|condition|no other condition will do|not IF: only if is the single way|Only if you practise can you win.
so that|purpose|the purpose of the main action|not BECAUSE: so that looks forward to a goal|Use can or may now, could or would for past.
in order that|purpose|a formal so that|not SO THAT in formal writing|Formal; may or might follows.
lest|purpose|so that something bad will not happen|not SO THAT: lest already holds a negative|Never add not after lest.
for fear that|purpose|because of a fear that|not SO THAT: for fear that shows worry|After it use should or might.
so ... that|result|so much of a quality that a result follows|not SUCH: so goes with an adjective alone|It was so hot that I fainted.
such ... that|result|so strong a thing that a result follows|not SO: such goes with a noun|It was such a hot day that we stayed in.
than|comparison|the thing compared, after a comparative|not THEN: than compares, then is time|Taller than, never taller then.
as ... as|comparison|shows that two things are equal|not THAN: equality needs AS ... AS|The first as may become so in a negative.
as if|comparison|how it seems: an unreal or seeming idea|not LIKE: before a clause use as if|Use were: as if he were rich.
as though|comparison|the same as as if|not LIKE before a clause|She looked as though she had seen a ghost.
the more ... the more|comparison|one thing grows as the other grows|not THAN: it is a pair with THE ... THE|The more you read, the more you learn.
where|place|in or to the place in which|not WHEN: where is about place|Stay where you are.
wherever|place|in any place|not WHERE: wherever means any place at all|One word.
as|manner|in the way that|not LIKE: before a clause use as|Do as I say.
how|manner|the way in which|not AS: how asks about the way|I do not know how he did it.
that|noun|introduces a statement|not WHETHER: that is for a statement, whether a question|He said that he was ready. That can be left out.
whether|noun|introduces a yes or no question inside a sentence|not THAT: whether means yes or no|Use whether with or not.
if|noun|introduces an indirect yes or no question|not WHETHER before or not: use whether|Whether is safer in formal writing.
both ... and|add|two things together, of equal weight|not EITHER: both means all of them|Keep both sides parallel.
between ... and|add|exactly two things, joined as a pair|not OR or TO: between needs AND|After between use me, him, her, never I, he, she.
either ... or|choice|one of two|not NEITHER: either affirms one|The verb agrees with the nearer subject.
neither ... nor|choice|not one and not the other|not EITHER: neither is negative|Add no other not.
not only ... but also|add|one thing, then a stronger one|not BOTH: not only adds with stress|Start with not only: the helper verb comes first.
whether ... or|choice|two possibilities, and it does not matter which|not EITHER: whether ... or is about not mattering|Whether you agree or not.
not ... but|contrast|says what it is not, then what it is|not NOT ONLY: no addition, a correction|Keep both parts the same kind of word.
rather ... than|choice|prefers the first to the second|not EITHER: rather shows a preference|I would rather walk than wait.
no sooner ... than|time|the second event at once after the first|not HARDLY ... THAN: no sooner takes than|Helper verb first: no sooner had he come.
hardly ... when|time|the second event at once after the first|not NO SOONER ... WHEN: hardly takes when|Hardly had she sat when the bell rang.
scarcely ... when|time|the second event at once after the first|not SCARCELY ... THAN: it takes when|Also scarcely ... before.
however|contrast|a contrast with the sentence before|not BUT: however links sentences, but joins parts|Semicolon or full stop before, comma after.
therefore|result|the result of what was said|not BECAUSE: therefore gives the result|Semicolon before, comma after.
moreover|add|adds a stronger or extra point|not ALSO: moreover adds a new, stronger point|Formal.
furthermore|add|adds one more point|not MOREOVER: the point is only extra, not stronger|Formal.
nevertheless|contrast|in spite of that|not HOWEVER: nevertheless stresses in spite of|Formal. Nonetheless is the same.
nonetheless|contrast|in spite of that|not THEREFORE: nonetheless goes against|Formal.
consequently|result|as a result|not THEREFORE: consequently stresses a chain of results|Formal.
thus|result|in this way, so|not SO: thus is formal and may mean in this way|Formal.
hence|result|for this reason (formal)|not THEREFORE: hence is shorter and goes with a noun|Short and formal.
accordingly|result|in a way that suits what was said|not THEREFORE: accordingly means in the suitable way|We acted accordingly.
meanwhile|time|at the same time, in another place|not WHILE: meanwhile joins sentences|The men cooked. Meanwhile, the women set the table.
otherwise|condition|if not: or else|not OR: otherwise is a sentence-linking warning|Leave now. Otherwise, you will be late.
instead|contrast|in place of that|not INSTEAD OF: instead stands alone|Instead is an adverb; instead of is a preposition.
besides|add|also, a lighter extra point|not BESIDE: beside means next to|Besides, I am tired.
also|add|too: another point|not MOREOVER: also is only another point|Also usually goes before the verb.
likewise|add|in the same way|not SIMILARLY: likewise says the same action|He voted. Likewise, his brother.
similarly|add|in a like way|not LIKEWISE: similarly compares two cases|Similarly, the south suffered.
in addition|add|and also: a formal extra point|not MOREOVER: in addition is plainly extra|In addition, the school needs a library.
on the other hand|contrast|the other side of one question|not BUT: it weighs two sides of one thing|Use it for two sides of one question.
in contrast|contrast|sets two things against each other|not HOWEVER: in contrast compares two things|The north is dry. In contrast, the south is wet.
on the contrary|contrast|the opposite is true|not HOWEVER: it denies the earlier statement|It denies what was just said.
for example|example|introduces one example|not SUCH AS: for example starts a sentence|For instance means the same.
for instance|example|introduces one example|not NAMELY: for instance gives only one|For instance, Japan.
in fact|emphasis|the truth is even stronger or different|not INDEED: in fact may correct what was said|He is not poor. In fact, he is rich.
indeed|emphasis|really, truly|not IN FACT: indeed only strengthens|It was cold; indeed, freezing.
as a result|result|because of this|not AS A RESULT OF: that one takes a noun|The crops failed. As a result, prices rose.
then|sequence|after that, next|not AND: then shows the order|Then is not a conjunction.
still|contrast|in spite of that|not YET: still is an adverb|It is late. Still, we should try.
afterwards|sequence|later, after that|not AFTER: afterwards stands alone|We ate. Afterwards, we walked.
finally|sequence|at last, the last point|not AT LAST: finally lists the last point|Finally, add the salt.
firstly|sequence|the first point|not AT FIRST: firstly opens a list|Firstly, the plan is cheap.
in conclusion|summary|to end, the last word|not FINALLY: in conclusion sums up everything|In conclusion, education is the key.
in short|summary|to say it briefly|not IN CONCLUSION: in short is a quick summary|In short, the plan failed.
in other words|summary|to say the same thing more clearly|not FOR EXAMPLE: it restates, not adds|He is frugal. In other words, careful.
that is|summary|to explain exactly|not IN OTHER WORDS: that is is short|He is a bachelor, that is, unmarried.
namely|example|to name the exact items|not FOR EXAMPLE: namely names all of them|Three provinces, namely Punjab, Sindh and Balochistan.
above all|emphasis|the most important point|not FIRSTLY: above all ranks the point|Above all, be honest.
"""
